{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 80,
    "dDurationMs": 500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Oh Hello There. I’m Matt from a different quantum timeline. I figured out the secret truth behind quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580,
    "dDurationMs": 12504,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mechanics and I’m sending it to Matt in\nyour timeline so he can tell you. Stand by."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17539,
    "dDurationMs": 5701,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Listen to the world around you for a moment… What do you hear? My voice, obviously."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23240,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No doubt a sublime subjective experience - but only subjective. Outside your skull, that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28650,
    "dDurationMs": 5690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sound is nothing but an expanding series of density waves - air molecules mindlessly bumping"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34340,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and shoving each other, oblivious to the complex wave structure that they propagate. And that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39340,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sound wave itself can be deconstructed into an overlapping set of simple sinusoidal waves"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45070,
    "dDurationMs": 4090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that move independently of each other, in\nexactly the combination of frequencies and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49160,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "amplitudes to encode me talking about them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53620,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there are other sounds - the background music, maybe your computer’s fan, or the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58220,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dishwasher, or the wind, birds, traffic. Each\nsound is its own configuration of overlapping"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63360,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sinusoidal waves. All these waves overlap\nto produce a fantastically complex bath of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69440,
    "dDurationMs": 6150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "density fluctuations. A snapshot of particle\npositions in the room would reveal a hopeless"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75590,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scramble. And yet somehow your ear and your brain’s audio processing network can pick"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81020,
    "dDurationMs": 2920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out and focus on each individual sound."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83940,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Everything I just described is real, but it’s\nalso an analogy for the quantum multiverse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89550,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A tenuous analogy - but bare with me. In a recent episode I showed you how overlapping"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94930,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "systems of ripples on a pond evolve independently of each other due to something called the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100140,
    "dDurationMs": 5990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "superposition principle. This principle also applies to the wavefunction in quantum mechanics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106130,
    "dDurationMs": 4450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the Many Worlds interpretation of quantum mechanics, the universal wavefunction is the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110580,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reality, encompassing all possible histories\nand futures and all exist. But we are only"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116300,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sensitive to a slice of the wavefunction corresponding to our “world”, and due to the superposition"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122540,
    "dDurationMs": 5969,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "principle our world can happily do its thing unperturbed by other parts of the wavefunction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128509,
    "dDurationMs": 4641,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- other “ripples”, or worlds. It’s as\nthough you were only sensitive to one source"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133150,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of sound - say, my voice - and your brain filtered out all the others. The presence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137730,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of those other sound waves has no impact on how my voice propagates."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142140,
    "dDurationMs": 7030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, cute analogy, but perhaps pointless because we don’t even know if Many Worlds is right."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149170,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are other ways to interpret the math\nof quantum mechanics that don’t require"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152950,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a multiverse. For example there’s the Copenhagen Interpretation, which says that the wavefunction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157980,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collapses at the point of measurement, leaving only one reality; or de Broglie-Bohm pilot"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163180,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wave theory, which says that particles are\nparticles and waves are waves - and the wavefunction’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167930,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "job is to shuttle actual real particles around - again, leading to one reality. And there"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173540,
    "dDurationMs": 2740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are quite a few others besides."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176280,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’re now approaching 100 years since the discovery of quantum mechanics, and we still"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181400,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "don’t know which of these - if any - are\nright. So what’s the holdup? A clue to the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185780,
    "dDurationMs": 5289,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problem lies in the word “interpretation”\n- an interpretation of quantum mechanics is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191069,
    "dDurationMs": 5681,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exactly that - it’s a story about what's\nreally happening behind the math - what “physical”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196750,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mechanisms give rise to the equations of quantum mechanics. And the fact is, every prominent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202140,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interpretation of quantum mechanics is perfectly consistent with the equation that lies at"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207230,
    "dDurationMs": 3580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the heart of the theory - that’s the Schrodinger equation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210810,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Schrodinger equation describes how the wavefunction of a quantum system changes over"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215670,
    "dDurationMs": 5270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space and time - and so it should completely determine the measurements we can make of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220940,
    "dDurationMs": 5980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that quantum system. But if our observations are 100% determined by the Schrodinger equation,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226920,
    "dDurationMs": 4649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and all interpretations give the same Schrodinger equation, then how can any measurement ever"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231569,
    "dDurationMs": 3670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tell between these interpretations?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235239,
    "dDurationMs": 6061,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It turns out there might be a way - but only\nif the Schrodinger equation is wrong. Well,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241300,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not wrong but incomplete. There are certain additional terms that we could add to the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245040,
    "dDurationMs": 4919,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Schrodinger equation that may have such a tiny influence that we haven’t noticed them"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249959,
    "dDurationMs": 6131,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before. But if they’re real we could distinguish between these interpretations. And much more than"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256090,
    "dDurationMs": 5369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that - they’d give us some pretty crazy\nscience fiction powers - I’m talking faster"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261459,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than light communication, and even the ability to send messages between the worlds of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266039,
    "dDurationMs": 4581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum multiverse - if it turns out that\nactually exists."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270620,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To understand all of this, let’s first go\nback to sound waves. As we discussed in that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275520,
    "dDurationMs": 4810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "previous episode, this ability for waves to\npass through each other without being scrambled"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280330,
    "dDurationMs": 5709,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is due to the superposition principle. Let’s\ndig a little deeper. This principle says that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286039,
    "dDurationMs": 4791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you can determine the evolution of multiple overlapping waves by calculating the evolution"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290830,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for each wave separately and then adding together the result. For that to be true, the medium"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296800,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "carrying the wave has to behave in a particular way - whether that medium is water, air, the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 302360,
    "dDurationMs": 6699,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fabric of spacetime itself. Waves can happen in any elastic medium - anything that tends"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309059,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to return to an equilibrium state after being\nstretched or displaced, because that can produce"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314129,
    "dDurationMs": 5490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an oscillation, and in which adjacent points\npull on each other, cause that can cause the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319619,
    "dDurationMs": 2100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "oscillation to travel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321719,
    "dDurationMs": 5961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the simplest imaginable case, the force\nthat tries to bring the medium back to equilibrium"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327680,
    "dDurationMs": 5049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is just proportional to the displacement at\neach point. That’s the case for the most"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332729,
    "dDurationMs": 6371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "idealized oscillation - the simple harmonic\noscillator. And that tends to be a good approximation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339100,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for any elastic medium as long as the displacement is small."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343570,
    "dDurationMs": 5799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The restoring force of a simple harmonic oscillator is what we call linear - which just means"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349369,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the output - the restoring force - is\nproportional to the input - the displacement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354629,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s what allows two overlapping displacements to be treated independently. A linear restoring"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361589,
    "dDurationMs": 5670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "force leads to a linear wave equation - and a linear wave equation is what you need for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367259,
    "dDurationMs": 2530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the superposition principle to be satisfied."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369789,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now in the physical world the superposition principle only holds to a point. Real pond"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374659,
    "dDurationMs": 6562,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surfaces or air density fields don’t behave like simple harmonic oscillators if you try to change"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381221,
    "dDurationMs": 6098,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "them by too much. Non-linearities creep in which can do things like damp the waves - cause"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387319,
    "dDurationMs": 1630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "them to lose energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388949,
    "dDurationMs": 5261,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the Schrodinger equation as we usually write it is a perfectly linear equation, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 394210,
    "dDurationMs": 5009,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in quantum mechanics it’s always assumed that linearity and the superposition principle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399219,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hold. Stack wavefunctions on top of each other and they behave as though the others aren’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403979,
    "dDurationMs": 3990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there. This gives us a sense of why it seems impossible to test the Many Worlds hypothesis"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407969,
    "dDurationMs": 6310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- those other worlds by definition have no\neffect on our own. That’s true as long as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414279,
    "dDurationMs": 6150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Schrodinger equation is perfectly linear. But here’s the rub: it turns out that if"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420429,
    "dDurationMs": 6610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Schrodinger equation has extra terms,\nhowever tiny, that are non-linear, then everything"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 427039,
    "dDurationMs": 5930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "changes. Not only can we test quantum interpretations, but we can do some things that really should"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432969,
    "dDurationMs": 1250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be impossible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434219,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was the Nobel laureate Steven Weinberg\nwho had the first insight. He realized that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439189,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even a tiny deviation from linearity in the Schrodinger equation would add extra non-linear"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444849,
    "dDurationMs": 5650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "observables to the wavefunction. The normal linear observables are things like position,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450499,
    "dDurationMs": 6030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "momentum, spin - the physical stuff that makes up our world. Extra observables would be non-local"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456529,
    "dDurationMs": 5111,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- they would exist across the entire wavefunction. And that, in principle, could give a way to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461640,
    "dDurationMs": 5029,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explore what happens to the wavefunction after\nmeasurement. Does it vanish as Copenhagen"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466669,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "demands, or persist as many worlds would have us believe?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470649,
    "dDurationMs": 4091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Weinberg’s fun little paper may have been\noverlooked if it hadn’t caught the attention"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474740,
    "dDurationMs": 6139,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of another brilliant physicist, Joseph Polchinski. In a single 1991 paper, Polchinski showed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480879,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how Weinberg’s “non-linear observables”\nwould make it possible to achieve some pretty"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485939,
    "dDurationMs": 2651,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "crazy science fiction effects."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488590,
    "dDurationMs": 4869,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First Polchinski showed that almost any non-linear\naddition to the Schrodinger equation would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493459,
    "dDurationMs": 4501,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mean that information could be sent between\nentangled pairs of particles. Now we’ve"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497960,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "been over entanglement before, but to remind you: if two particles are entangled then their"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502870,
    "dDurationMs": 6150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "properties are correlated. By choosing how\nto measure one of the pair, you influence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509020,
    "dDurationMs": 6070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the state of its partner - essentially instantaneously\nand over any distance. However the nature"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515090,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of this influence makes it impossible to send actual information this way. You can only"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519890,
    "dDurationMs": 5290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "detect that the influence happened by comparing the measurement statistics of multiple entangled"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525180,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pairs - and to do that you need to send regular, sub-light-speed information. It’s almost"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531020,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like the universe conspires to prevent any\nsuperluminal effects."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535410,
    "dDurationMs": 7740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in exactly 11 lines of math, Polchinski shows that this conspiracy is delicate. Almost ANY"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543150,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deviation from perfect linearity in the Schrodinger equation would make it possible to send real"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549120,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "information between entangled pairs of particles, enabling instant communication at any distance,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555480,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and even backwards in time. Now Polchinski doesn’t actually tell us how to do this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560300,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- he only proves that it should be possible\nin principle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563520,
    "dDurationMs": 6380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But he was only getting started. He follows\nup by finding a way to write one non-linear"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569900,
    "dDurationMs": 6460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Schrodinger equation that avoids the causality-breaking prospect of faster-than-light communication."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576360,
    "dDurationMs": 6530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it turns out that in doing so he  stumbles upon a way to communicate between the worlds of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582890,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum multiverse. And this time he actually tells us how to do it, inventing what he calls the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589190,
    "dDurationMs": 4210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Everett-Wheeler telephone, after Hugh Everett - the guy who came up with the Many Worlds"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593400,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interpretation, and John Archibald Wheeler, Everett’s graduate advisor."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597580,
    "dDurationMs": 3730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let me run you through it. We’re going to\nuse a Stern-Gerlach device - something we’ve"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601310,
    "dDurationMs": 4060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "talked about a bunch. Basically it’s a pair\nof magnets - a north and south pole - that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605370,
    "dDurationMs": 5350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deflect particles with spin and charge. It\nmeasures the direction of spin by whether"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610720,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the particles are deflected to the north or\nsouth pole. Quantum particles will always"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615640,
    "dDurationMs": 5270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be found to have a spin in the direction that you choose to align the magnets. So your choice"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620910,
    "dDurationMs": 6890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "affects the quantum wavefunction. Polchinski lays out the steps very clearly: you send"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 627800,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a spin half particle like an electron through\na Stern-Gerlach device and then you measure"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632910,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the direction of the spin. It has to be pointing either to the north or south poles - we’ll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 638230,
    "dDurationMs": 6470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "call them up or down. In the many-worlds interpretation, by making that measurement you just split"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644700,
    "dDurationMs": 6500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the world in two and you split yourself. In\none world you measure spin down - we’ll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651200,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "call that spin-down you … you. In the other\nworld, other you will measure spin up - we’ll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658360,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "call spin-up you “other you”. So now you\nwill now try to send a message to other you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665040,
    "dDurationMs": 5990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First, you, but not other you, need to inject\nsome information into the electron’s wavefunction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671030,
    "dDurationMs": 4541,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You’ll do that by making a choice: either\nyou leave the electron with spin-down, or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675571,
    "dDurationMs": 5589,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you rotate it to spin-up. After that, both\nyou’s send their version of the electron"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 681160,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to some hypothetical and perhaps impossible\ndevice that subjects both branches of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685640,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electron wavefunction to a non-linear field.\nThat field sort of spreads the local information"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692240,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from each branch - each world - through the entire electron wavefunction. Finally, the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697660,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electrons go back through the Stern-Gerlach\ndevice and other you measures the spin once"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702050,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "again. If you chose to rotate your electron\nfrom down to up, other you will find their"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 708070,
    "dDurationMs": 5870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electron rotated from up to down. If you did\nnothing, other you will also find no change."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713940,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You’ve now successfully transmitted a single bit of information between quantum timelines."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720460,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The real math is quite a bit more complicated, but I've given you a sense of it. In order to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725850,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "build an actual telephone you probably want to send more than a single bit. Unfortunately"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730320,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you can’t just use more electrons, because\neach electron further splits the worlds - you’ll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735340,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just be sending a single bit to more you’s.\nPolchinski’s idea really just serves as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741400,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a proof of concept that in a non-linear quantum mechanics, actions can influence the entire"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747760,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wavefunction - spanning different “worlds”.\nPerhaps real communication would be possible,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753560,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "however there’s one hard limitation - you\ncan only talk to worlds created by the action"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758570,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the telephone itself. I’m afraid that\nthe world where you made all of those better"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763250,
    "dDurationMs": 3890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decisions about your life remains forever\nout of reach."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767140,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK to summarize: either quantum mechanics is perfectly linear and you should forget I said anything,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773940,
    "dDurationMs": 7510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OR it’s nonlinear and we can instantly communicate across any distance and back in time, OR we can communicate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781450,
    "dDurationMs": 6220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "across the branches of the quantum multiverse. According to Polchinski exactly one and only"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787670,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one of those must be true. Perhaps there’s a me on a different timeline, or in the future,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 794190,
    "dDurationMs": 7130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who’s smart enough to figure all of this out and is now sending me a message. I"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 801320,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "guess he chose a different me. There are,\nafter all, many worlds in the greater Everettian"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806500,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 820901,
    "dDurationMs": 5389,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we get to comments, we wanted to let you know that if you’re looking for a fun"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 826290,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "show for the young scientists in your lives,\nthan you should check out MEGAWOW on the PBS"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 831590,
    "dDurationMs": 3053,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kids Youtube Channel. This show is designed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834643,
    "dDurationMs": 5074,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to get kids excited about science through\nfun experiments. If you check it out, remember"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839717,
    "dDurationMs": 2532,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to tell them, politely, that Space Time sent\nyou!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842249,
    "dDurationMs": 5084,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Last episode we talked about this one very\nweird white dwarf star that scientists think"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847333,
    "dDurationMs": 4997,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may be the first observation of the result\nof the merger of two white dwarfs - and how"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852330,
    "dDurationMs": 4801,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this may have huge implications for all of\ncosmology. Let’s see what you had to say"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857131,
    "dDurationMs": 8289,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tom MS asks how can you rule out that this white dwarf got its weird properties like its rapid rotation from being"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 865420,
    "dDurationMs": 5861,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the result of a merger, rather than being a more usual white dwarf accreting from a partner star"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871281,
    "dDurationMs": 5052,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before being ejected from that binary system. OK, so Tom has a couple of good insights here"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876333,
    "dDurationMs": 5999,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- first is that a white dwarf could potentially gain a lot of angular momentum or spin by absorbing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882332,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "material from another star, hence perhaps\nexplaining that high spin. And the second is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 886952,
    "dDurationMs": 4515,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that, in order for that explanation to be\nvalid we’d need to know what happened to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 891467,
    "dDurationMs": 2236,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the other star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893703,
    "dDurationMs": 5666,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s turn to another comment by awuma to help answer this. Awuma tells us that such"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899369,
    "dDurationMs": 6093,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rapidly rotating white dwarfs are found in\npost-common envelope close binary systems"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905462,
    "dDurationMs": 8029,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- V471 Tauri with its 7 minute rotation being a good example. For those non-white-dwarf-astrophysicists out"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913491,
    "dDurationMs": 7180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there, a common envelope binary system is one where the stars are so close together that they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920671,
    "dDurationMs": 3561,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "share an envelope - you can also describe that as saying he white dwarf orbits inside"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 924232,
    "dDurationMs": 6511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the other star. Now V471 is a post-common-envelope system, meaning the white dwarf presumably"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 930743,
    "dDurationMs": 4917,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ate up the surrounding envelope, leaving behind a red dwarf."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935660,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So this tells me that yes, you can greatly\nspin up a white dwarf by feeding it the envelope"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941280,
    "dDurationMs": 4864,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of its binary partner. But is there a way\nto then get rid of the rest of that partner?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946144,
    "dDurationMs": 5703,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, one possibility is that the partner\ngoes on to explode as a supernova. But the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 951847,
    "dDurationMs": 5839,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass transfer onto the white dwarf actually makes this less likely because losing mass"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957686,
    "dDurationMs": 6229,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reduces the internal pressure, the fusion rate, etc. You can actually defuse a potential future"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 963915,
    "dDurationMs": 6039,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supernova by sucking away its outer layers. The question is the can you absorb just enough"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 969954,
    "dDurationMs": 5586,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gas to spin up the white dwarf while still\nallowing the partner star to explode? Well, I'm not"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 975540,
    "dDurationMs": 6123,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sure - but it seems these scientists think that, that's less likely that the white dwarf collision explanation,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 981663,
    "dDurationMs": 5398,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which at any rate we know must happen at least sometimes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 987061,
    "dDurationMs": 4477,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cyber Persona asks what happens to the star’s magnetic fields after it goes supernova. And"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 991538,
    "dDurationMs": 6015,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then guesses the correct answer - it dissipates into space with the matter, potentially leading"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 997553,
    "dDurationMs": 5714,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to electromagnetic radiation. And that is exactly it. The supernova shock front is a mixture"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1003267,
    "dDurationMs": 4991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of high energy particles and magnetic fields. Those magnetic fields do lots of things - including"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1008258,
    "dDurationMs": 4569,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accelerating particles to even higher energies. This is believed to be one source of cosmic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1012827,
    "dDurationMs": 5345,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rays that reach the earth. The magnetic fields then go on to add to the galaxy’s magnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1018172,
    "dDurationMs": 5236,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We see that field in many ways, including by watching the radio light emitted by electrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1023408,
    "dDurationMs": 4958,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spiraling in that magnetic field - what we\ncall synchrotron radiation. Galactic magnetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1028366,
    "dDurationMs": 4342,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fields have very clear bubble-like structures\nthat come from past supernovae."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1032708,
    "dDurationMs": 5029,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now I started that episode with a mangled quote - scientific progress is accompanied not by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1037737,
    "dDurationMs": 5202,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cries of “Eureka”, but instead by murmurs of “huh, that’s weird”. Vingador das"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1042939,
    "dDurationMs": 4724,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Estrelas pointed out that the more common version is “The most exciting phrase to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1047663,
    "dDurationMs": 3651,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hear in science, the one that heralds new\ndiscoveries, is not 'Eureka!' but 'That's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1051314,
    "dDurationMs": 6552,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "funny…” attributed to Isaac Asimov. Turns out the true origin is probably even more obscure"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1057866,
    "dDurationMs": 6943,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- It appears in the 1976 textbook Introduction to the History of Mycology by G. C. Ainsworth,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1064809,
    "dDurationMs": 5554,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "recounting Alexander Fleming’s discovery\nof the bacteria-killing power of penicillin"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1070363,
    "dDurationMs": 6884,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- upon noticing the “lysis of the staphylococci” his cry of Eureka was “that’s funny”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1077247,
    "dDurationMs": 7765,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Asimov attribution first appeared in fortune, a random quote generator program"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1085012,
    "dDurationMs": 7772,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in UNIX - so a totally reliable source. I\ngot the real answer from this from the quote investigator"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1092784,
    "dDurationMs": 6777,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "web site, but not before spending … way\ntoo long trying to find out. The real lesson"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1099561,
    "dDurationMs": 4988,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is don’t get stuck down quote verification rabbit holes. Which apparently I’m never"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1104549,
    "dDurationMs": 6094,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "going to learn. Of course as Einstein said, \"The definition of insanity is doing the same thing over "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1110643,
    "dDurationMs": 7536,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and over and expecting different results.” Which, by the way, is also a misattribution - that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1118179,
    "dDurationMs": 4107,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was in the 1981 book produced by narcotics anonymous. Dammit. See what I mean?"
    } ]
  } ]
}
